<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarvajna Singabhupala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century</span> · Also known as Singabhupala</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarvajna Singabhupala was a ruler of the Recherla <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velama">Velama</a>) dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rachakonda">Rachakonda</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> in the 14th century. A noted patron and scholar, he wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasarnava-sudhakara">Rasarnava Sudhakara</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> treatise on dramaturgy and poetics still studied today. His court supported Telugu and Sanskrit writers, and his title Sarvajna means 'all-knowing'.</p></div>
